          "reasoning_content": "Interesting. Key observations:\n- Alert: log_loss observed 25.23 vs reference 0.507, threshold 0.541. Huge spike.\n- Context: performance history shows focus window and confirmation-1 both with high log_loss (~25, ~23.9). Accuracy low ~0.29-0.33, but recall very high ~0.98, precision low ~0.27-0.31, ROC AUC ~0.504 (essentially random). Confusion matrix: focus: [[14, 353],[3, 130]] — so TN=14, FP=353, FN=3, TP=130. That means the model predicts churn=1 for almost everyone (353+130=483 positives out of 500). So the model is predicting churn for nearly everyone, with probability mean 0.9676, median 1.0.\n\nWait, confusion matrix [[14, 353],[3, 130]]: rows are actual (0,1), columns predicted (0,1)? TN=14 (actual 0 pred 0), FP=353 (actual 0 pred 1), FN=3 (actual 1 pred 0), TP=130 (actual 1 pred 1). Total actual 0 = 14+353=367, actual 1 = 3+130=133. So churn rate in feedback is 133/500 = 0.266, matches mean 0.266. Predicted positives = 353+130 = 483 out of 500 = 0.966, matches prediction mean 0.966.\n\nSo the model predicts churn=1 for 96.6% of customers. Probability mean 0.9676. This is a model that's gone wild — predicting near-certain churn for everyone. ROC AUC ~0.5 (random), log loss huge because it assigns high probability to churn for many actual non-churners.\n\n- model_catalog: reference_model and active_model have the SAME version hash sha256:cbf03ab... So no version change between reference and active. So compare_model_versions would show no difference (same model). So deployment_regression via version change is unlikely — but wait, predictions show model_version = that same hash for all 500. So the active model serving is the same as reference.\n\nHmm. But the model predicts everyone as churn. That's strange. If the model is the same as reference, why is it predicting everyone as churn now? Possibly because the INPUTS received by the model are corrupted/shifted such that the model outputs high probability for everyone.\n\nLet me compare source vs inputs — are they the same? The source is \"capture before transformations\", inputs is \"data effectively received by model\". If they differ, there's a transformation/deployment issue. Let me compare_tables source vs inputs.\n\nAlso compare_distributions source vs reference_clients to check data drift. And compare_predictions / evaluate_predictions to get exact metrics.\n\nLet me think about what's most diagnostic:\n1. compare_tables(source, inputs) — are the data the model received identical to source? If inputs are corrupted (e.g., all monthly_charges high, or some feature shifted), that could cause the model to predict churn for everyone.\n2. evaluate_predictions(predictions, feedback) — confirm log_loss ~25.23.\n3. compare_distributions(source, reference_clients) — drift in features.\n4. compare_conditional_relationship — concept drift.\n\nThe massive shift to predicting everyone as churn with the SAME model version strongly suggests the inputs received by the model are corrupted (data_corruption) OR there's data drift pushing all inputs into a region where the model predicts churn.\n\nActually, log_loss of 25 with probabilities near 1 for actual 0s: log loss contribution for actual 0 with p=1 is -log(1-1) = -log(0) = inf. But they cap probabilities. Mean probability 0.9676, min 0.0285. For actual 0 customers (367 of them), most got p near 1 → huge log loss. That's the driver.\n\nWhy would the same model suddenly predict p≈1 for everyone? Either inputs changed (data_corruption in the pipeline feeding the model) or the model itself is broken. Since reference and active model are identical version, compare_model_versions would show identical performance — pointing away from model regression and toward input data corruption.\n\nLet me verify by comparing source vs inputs. If they're identical, then the inputs aren't corrupted and the issue is the model acting on same inputs differently — but model is same version... then it'd be concept drift or the reference isn't actually comparable. Let me check.\n\nLet me run:\n- compare_tables(left=source, right=inputs) — key\n- evaluate_predictions() — confirm metrics\n- compare_distributions(left=source, right=reference_clients) — drift\n\nThese are independent, batch them.\n\nActually compare_tables source vs inputs: if identical, returns 0 changed. Let me do it.\n\nLet me also read the full context to see confirmation-2 metrics, and read metrics.json and confirmation metrics. But context text was cut off at 1250 chars. Let me get the rest of context and the confirmation metrics. Actually, I have enough from the alert and context head. Let me focus.\n\nLet me batch:\n1. compare_tables(source, inputs) key=customer_id\n2. evaluate_predictions()  (default)\n3. compare_distributions(source, reference_clients) — columns = the feature columns\n\nLet me do these three in parallel.",
